+    <!-- Section: History / Overview -->
+    <section class="bollywood-section" id="overview">
+      <div class="bollywood-container bollywood-grid-2col">
+        <div class="bollywood-text-block">
+          <span class="bollywood-eyebrow">Industry Roots &amp; Legacy</span>
+          <h2>A Century of Hindi Cinema</h2>
+          <p>Bollywood — a name blending Bombay and Hollywood — refers to the Hindi-language film industry headquartered in Mumbai. It is the largest film industry in the world by the number of films produced annually, and its cultural reach extends across South Asia and the global diaspora.</p>
+          <p>Dadasaheb Phalke's <em>Raja Harishchandra</em> (1913) is widely regarded as India's first full-length feature film, earning Phalke the title "Father of Indian Cinema." Sound arrived with Ardeshir Irani's <em>Alam Ara</em> (1931), India's first talkie, launching a Hindi-language studio era that would grow into a global cultural export.</p>
+        </div>
+        <div class="bollywood-highlight-box">
+          <h3>&#127902;&#65039; At a Glance</h3>
+          <ul>
+            <li><strong>First feature film:</strong> Raja Harishchandra (1913), directed by Dadasaheb Phalke.</li>
+            <li><strong>First talkie:</strong> Alam Ara (1931), directed by Ardeshir Irani.</li>
+            <li><strong>Industry hub:</strong> Mumbai (Film City, Andheri &amp; the historic studio lots of the 1930s–50s).</li>
+            <li><strong>Output:</strong> Among the highest annual film output of any industry in the world, spanning romance, action, drama, and musical genres.</li>
+          </ul>
+        </div>
+      </div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- Section: Famous Movies -->
+    <section class="bollywood-section" id="films">
+      <div class="bollywood-container">
+        <div class="bollywood-section-header">
+          <span class="bollywood-eyebrow">Landmark Films</span>
+          <h2>Famous Movies of Bollywood</h2>
+          <p>The films that defined eras, broke box-office records, and shaped Hindi cinema's storytelling language.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="bollywood-card-grid">
+          <div class="bollywood-card">
+            <span class="bollywood-card-icon">&#127916;</span>
+            <h3>Alam Ara (1931)</h3>
+            <p>Directed by Ardeshir Irani, India's first talkie. Its arrival marked the beginning of the Hindi sound-film era and the birth of playback-driven musical storytelling.</p>
+          </div>
+          <div class="bollywood-card">
+            <span class="bollywood-card-icon">&#127806;</span>
+            <h3>Mother India (1957)</h3>
+            <p>Directed by Mehboob Khan, this epic drama became India's first submission to earn an Academy Award nomination for Best Foreign Language Film.</p>
+          </div>
+          <div class="bollywood-card">
+            <span class="bollywood-card-icon">&#128299;</span>
+            <h3>Sholay (1975)</h3>
+            <p>Directed by Ramesh Sippy, this action-adventure "curry western" is frequently cited as the most influential Hindi film ever made, its dialogue still quoted decades later.</p>
+          </div>
+          <div class="bollywood-card">
+            <span class="bollywood-card-icon">&#128142;</span>
+            <h3>Dilwale Dulhania Le Jayenge (1995)</h3>
+            <p>Directed by Aditya Chopra, this romance became a cultural phenomenon and holds the record for the longest continuous theatrical run in Indian cinema history.</p>
+          </div>
+          <div class="bollywood-card">
+            <span class="bollywood-card-icon">&#127942;</span>
+            <h3>Lagaan (2001)</h3>
+            <p>Directed by Ashutosh Gowariker, this period sports drama earned an Academy Award nomination for Best Foreign Language Film, one of only three Hindi films to do so.</p>
+          </div>
+          <div class="bollywood-card">
+            <span class="bollywood-card-icon">&#129509;</span>
+            <h3>Dangal (2016)</h3>
+            <p>Directed by Nitesh Tiwari and starring Aamir Khan, this sports drama became one of the highest-grossing Indian films worldwide, especially popular across China.</p>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- Section: Legendary Actors -->
+    <section class="bollywood-section" id="actors">
+      <div class="bollywood-container">
+        <div class="bollywood-section-header">
+          <span class="bollywood-eyebrow">Silver Screen Icons</span>
+          <h2>Legendary Actors of Bollywood</h2>
+          <p>The performers who became household names and shaped Hindi cinema across generations.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="bollywood-card-grid">
+          <div class="bollywood-card">
+            <span class="bollywood-card-icon">&#127775;</span>
+            <h3>Dilip Kumar</h3>
+            <p>Known as the "Tragedy King," his naturalistic acting style in the 1940s-60s influenced generations of performers and earned him a record number of Filmfare Best Actor awards.</p>
+          </div>
+          <div class="bollywood-card">
+            <span class="bollywood-card-icon">&#128081;</span>
+            <h3>Amitabh Bachchan</h3>
+            <p>Dubbed the "Angry Young Man" of 1970s cinema, his commanding screen presence across five decades made him one of the most recognisable figures in Indian popular culture.</p>
+          </div>
+          <div class="bollywood-card">
+            <span class="bollywood-card-icon">&#127917;</span>
+            <h3>Sridevi</h3>
+            <p>A pan-Indian superstar celebrated for her range and screen charisma, she remains one of the most acclaimed actresses in the history of Hindi and South Indian cinema.</p>
+          </div>
+          <div class="bollywood-card">
+            <span class="bollywood-card-icon">&#9889;</span>
+            <h3>Shah Rukh Khan</h3>
+            <p>Known as the "King of Bollywood," his romantic-hero roles in the 1990s and 2000s made him one of the most commercially successful actors in film history by audience reach.</p>
+          </div>
+          <div class="bollywood-card">
+            <span class="bollywood-card-icon">&#127919;</span>
+            <h3>Aamir Khan</h3>
+            <p>Known for selective, socially conscious roles in films like Lagaan and Dangal, he is celebrated for pushing Hindi cinema toward global box-office milestones.</p>
+          </div>
+          <div class="bollywood-card">
+            <span class="bollywood-card-icon">&#128142;</span>
+            <h3>Deepika Padukone</h3>
+            <p>A leading actress of the contemporary era, recognised for a range spanning period epics to intimate dramas, and a prominent voice for mental-health awareness in India.</p>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- Section: Major Directors -->
+    <section class="bollywood-section" id="directors">
+      <div class="bollywood-container bollywood-grid-2col">
+        <div class="bollywood-highlight-box" style="background: rgba(200, 30, 78, 0.06); border-color: rgba(200, 30, 78, 0.22);">
+          <h3>&#127916; Visionary Directors</h3>
+          <ul>
+            <li><strong>Dadasaheb Phalke:</strong> Directed Raja Harishchandra (1913), the founding moment of Indian feature filmmaking.</li>
+            <li><strong>Raj Kapoor:</strong> Actor-director whose 1950s films carried Indian cinema's popularity across the Soviet Union and the Middle East.</li>
+            <li><strong>Guru Dutt:</strong> Celebrated for poetic, visually innovative dramas that are now considered landmarks of Indian art cinema.</li>
+            <li><strong>Yash Chopra:</strong> Known as the "King of Romance," his films across four decades defined the modern Bollywood love story.</li>
+            <li><strong>Mira Nair:</strong> Internationally acclaimed for bridging Indian and global storytelling traditions, including Monsoon Wedding and Salaam Bombay!</li>
+            <li><strong>Zoya Akhtar:</strong> A leading voice of contemporary Bollywood, known for socially attuned, character-driven ensemble films.</li>
+          </ul>
+        </div>
+        <div class="bollywood-text-block">
+          <span class="bollywood-eyebrow">Masters Behind the Camera</span>
+          <h2>Directors Who Shaped Hindi Cinema</h2>
+          <p>Bollywood's directors have ranged from studio-era pioneers who built the industry's grammar of song-and-story, to contemporary filmmakers pushing it toward global co-productions and streaming audiences.</p>
+          <p>Guru Dutt's visual poetry and Raj Kapoor's showman instincts defined the mid-century era, while Yash Chopra's romances shaped the 1990s. Today, directors like Zoya Akhtar and Mira Nair continue to widen the industry's storytelling range on the world stage.</p>
+        </div>
+      </div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- Section: Music Composers -->
+    <section class="bollywood-section" id="composers">
+      <div class="bollywood-container">
+        <div class="bollywood-section-header">
+          <span class="bollywood-eyebrow">The Sound of Bollywood</span>
+          <h2>Music Composers of Bollywood</h2>
+          <p>Music has always been central to Hindi cinema's identity — film soundtracks routinely top the charts independent of the films themselves.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="bollywood-card-grid">
+          <div class="bollywood-card">
+            <span class="bollywood-card-icon">&#127925;</span>
+            <h3>Lata Mangeshkar</h3>
+            <p>The most celebrated playback singer in Indian film history, her voice defined the sound of Hindi cinema across seven decades and thousands of songs.</p>
+          </div>
+          <div class="bollywood-card">
+            <span class="bollywood-card-icon">&#127928;</span>
+            <h3>R.D. Burman</h3>
+            <p>A composer renowned for fusing Western orchestration and rhythm with Hindi film music, his scores from the 1970s–80s remain widely sampled and remixed today.</p>
+          </div>
+          <div class="bollywood-card">
+            <span class="bollywood-card-icon">&#127911;</span>
+            <h3>A.R. Rahman</h3>
+            <p>A two-time Academy Award winner for Slumdog Millionaire, Rahman's genre-blending scores brought Indian film music to an international audience.</p>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- Section: Timeline -->
+    <section class="bollywood-section" id="timeline">
+      <div class="bollywood-container">
+        <div class="bollywood-section-header">
+          <span class="bollywood-eyebrow">Evolution</span>
+          <h2>Timeline of Hindi Cinema</h2>
+          <p>From a silent-era experiment to a globally recognised entertainment industry.</p>
+        </div>
+
+        <div class="bollywood-timeline">
+          <div class="bollywood-timeline-step">
+            <div class="bollywood-timeline-marker"></div>
+            <div class="bollywood-timeline-card">
+              <span class="bollywood-timeline-year">1913</span>
+              <h3>Birth of Indian Feature Film</h3>
+              <p>Dadasaheb Phalke directs <em>Raja Harishchandra</em>, widely regarded as India's first full-length feature film, earning him the title "Father of Indian Cinema."</p>
+            </div>
+          </div>
+          <div class="bollywood-timeline-step">
+            <div class="bollywood-timeline-marker"></div>
+            <div class="bollywood-timeline-card">
+              <span class="bollywood-timeline-year">1931</span>
+              <h3>The Talkie Era Begins</h3>
+              <p>Ardeshir Irani's <em>Alam Ara</em> becomes India's first sound film, launching the song-driven storytelling format that still defines Hindi cinema.</p>
+            </div>
+          </div>
+          <div class="bollywood-timeline-step">
+            <div class="bollywood-timeline-marker"></div>
+            <div class="bollywood-timeline-card">
+              <span class="bollywood-timeline-year">1940s&ndash;1960s</span>
+              <h3>The Golden Age</h3>
+              <p>Raj Kapoor, Dilip Kumar, and Guru Dutt define an era of studio-built stardom, poetic realism, and songs that carried Indian cinema's popularity abroad.</p>
+            </div>
+          </div>
+          <div class="bollywood-timeline-step">
+            <div class="bollywood-timeline-marker"></div>
+            <div class="bollywood-timeline-card">
+              <span class="bollywood-timeline-year">1975</span>
+              <h3>Sholay &amp; the Masala Blockbuster</h3>
+              <p><em>Sholay</em>'s release cements the "masala" formula — action, comedy, romance, and music in one film — as Bollywood's dominant commercial template.</p>
+            </div>
+          </div>
+          <div class="bollywood-timeline-step">
+            <div class="bollywood-timeline-marker"></div>
+            <div class="bollywood-timeline-card">
+              <span class="bollywood-timeline-year">1990s</span>
+              <h3>The Romance Era &amp; Global Diaspora</h3>
+              <p>Shah Rukh Khan's rise and films like Dilwale Dulhania Le Jayenge (1995) turn Bollywood romances into a defining cultural export for the South Asian diaspora.</p>
+            </div>
+          </div>
+          <div class="bollywood-timeline-step">
+            <div class="bollywood-timeline-marker"></div>
+            <div class="bollywood-timeline-card">
+              <span class="bollywood-timeline-year">2001&ndash;2016</span>
+              <h3>Global Recognition</h3>
+              <p>Lagaan (2001) earns an Oscar nomination; Slumdog Millionaire's A.R. Rahman score wins two Academy Awards; Dangal (2016) becomes a worldwide box-office hit.</p>
+            </div>
+          </div>
+          <div class="bollywood-timeline-step">
+            <div class="bollywood-timeline-marker"></div>
+            <div class="bollywood-timeline-card">
+              <span class="bollywood-timeline-year">2020s</span>
+              <h3>The Streaming Era</h3>
+              <p>OTT platforms reshape distribution and storytelling, widening Bollywood's genre range and bringing Hindi cinema to new global audiences via streaming.</p>
+            </div>
+          </div>
+        </div>
+      </div>
+    </section>
+
+    <!-- Section: Awards -->
+    <section class="bollywood-section" id="awards">
